acceptance_criteria: "Absolute-pattern detection uses glob syntax (a leading / or a drive prefix), never win32 path.isAbsolute on the raw pattern; tests/unit/core/fs/file-glob.spec.ts passes on windows-latest, with the leading-backslash parity entry POSIX-only and a cross-platform escape test; The Windows nightly shard that runs file-glob.spec.ts is green"
parent: pm-i7w2
risk: medium
confidence: high
severity: medium
resolution: "isAbsolutePattern in src/core/fs/file-glob.ts treats a pattern as absolute only for a leading / (root or //server/share UNC) or, on win32, a drive root C:/. splitStatic and literalMatch use it instead of win32 path.isAbsolute, so \\a.ts matches a.ts and \\!x.ts matches !x.ts relative to cwd on Windows as on POSIX. The tinyglobby parity entry for \\a.ts is POSIX-only (on Windows the old expansion matched nothing, a subset), and a cross-platform escape spec covers isAbsolutePattern on win32, linux and darwin."
expected_result: "A pattern with a leading backslash is read as a glob escape on every platform, with the same relative output on Windows and POSIX"
actual_result: "file-glob.spec.ts passes 17/17 on Linux, including the cross-platform escape spec; judging absoluteness with path.win32.isAbsolute fails it; the Windows proof is the PR's Windows regression (Node 24) job and the next nightly"
