notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-19T06:03:12.127Z","harness:claude-code","Implemented as proposed. isAbsolutePattern in src/core/fs/file-glob.ts treats a pattern as absolute only when it has a leading / (a root or a //server/share UNC root) or, on win32, a drive root matching ^[A-Za-z]:/. splitStatic and literalMatch both use it instead of path.isAbsolute, so a leading backslash is always the glob escape: \\a.ts matches a.ts and \\!x.ts matches !x.ts, with relative output on every platform. The tinyglobby parity entry \\a.ts is now POSIX-only, because on Windows the old expansion matched nothing and the escape reading is a superset. A new cross-platform spec covers the escape plus isAbsolutePattern on win32, linux and darwin. Behaviour that does not change: on win32, path.isAbsolute also treated C:\\x as absolute, but in glob grammar that pattern reads C: plus an escaped x, and both readings match no file because Windows file names cannot contain ':'. A POSIX directory named C: stays relative. Verified on Linux (17/17 in file-glob.spec.ts); the Windows proof is the PR's windows-latest matrix and the next nightly."
